---
import DocsShell from '../../components/DocsShell.astro'
import Code from '../../components/Code.astro'
import { href } from '../../lib/paths'

const installScript = `
curl -fsSL https://sparshlabs.com/ownci/install.sh | sh
`

const installRelease = `
sha256sum -c --ignore-missing SHA256SUMS        # must print: ownci_<version>_linux_<arch>.tar.gz: OK
tar -xzf ownci_<version>_linux_<arch>.tar.gz
install -m 0755 ownci ~/.local/bin/ownci        # make sure ~/.local/bin is on your PATH
ownci version
`

const installSource = `
git clone https://github.com/ashfaqc137/ownci.git
cd ownci
make build                                      # builds bin/ownci
install -m 0755 bin/ownci ~/.local/bin/ownci
`

const quickStart = `
ownci doctor                              # everything PASS?
ownci setup --repo owner/name             # downloads + verifies the runner, registers it, installs isolation
ownci start --detach                      # runs in the background (without --detach: foreground, Ctrl-C stops)
ownci status --remote                     # online, idle
`

const workflow = `
jobs:
  build:
    runs-on: [ownci]            # the opt-in label OwnCI registers (not "self-hosted")
    container: ubuntu:24.04     # mandatory: jobs without a container are rejected
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
      - run: make test
`

const stopping = `
ownci stop                                # stop taking jobs; registration and files are kept
ownci start --detach                      # the same registration comes back
ownci uninstall --unregister --purge      # remove it from GitHub and delete OwnCI's files
`
---

<DocsShell
  title="Get started"
  description="Install OwnCI, check your machine, register a runner for your repository and run your first job on it."
>
  <section class="docs-section" id="requirements">
    <p class="section-kicker">Step 1</p>
    <h2>Requirements</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Linux or WSL2</strong> on amd64 or arm64, and a <strong>non-root</strong> user (the runner refuses root). Native Windows and macOS are not supported yet.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Docker</strong> with a Linux daemon that your user can reach.
        On WSL2 with Docker Desktop, enable your distro under <em>Settings → Resources → WSL integration</em>.
        On native Linux, <a href="https://docs.docker.com/engine/install/" rel="noreferrer">install Docker Engine</a> and add yourself to the <code>docker</code> group.
      </li>
      <li><strong>ICU</strong> for the runner: <code>sudo apt-get install -y libicu-dev</code> on Debian/Ubuntu, <code>sudo dnf install libicu</code> on Fedora.</li>
      <li>
        A <strong>GitHub token with admin access to the repository</strong>, only for <code>setup</code>, <code>uninstall --unregister</code> and <code>status --remote</code>. OwnCI looks at <code>GITHUB_TOKEN</code>, then <code>GH_TOKEN</code>, then <code>gh auth login</code>. A fine-grained
        token needs <em>Administration: Read and write</em> on the repository. The token is never stored.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="docs-section" id="install">
    <p class="section-kicker">Step 2</p>
    <h2>Install</h2>
    <h3>With the install script</h3>
    <Code label="shell" code={installScript} />
    <p>
      The script downloads the latest release for your architecture, checks it against the release's <code>SHA256SUMS</code> and installs <code>ownci</code> into <code>~/.local/bin</code>.
      <code>OWNCI_VERSION</code> pins a version and <code>OWNCI_INSTALL_DIR</code> picks another directory, e.g. <code>… | OWNCI_VERSION=v0.1.0 sh</code>.
    </p>
    <h3>From a release, by hand</h3>
    <p>
      Download the archive for your architecture and <code>SHA256SUMS</code> from the <a href="https://github.com/ashfaqc137/ownci/releases" rel="noreferrer">Releases page</a>, then verify and install it:
    </p>
    <Code label="shell" code={installRelease} />
    <p>
      Each archive carries a GitHub build-provenance attestation: <code>gh attestation verify ownci_&lt;version&gt;_linux_&lt;arch&gt;.tar.gz --repo ashfaqc137/ownci</code>.
    </p>
    <h3>From source</h3>
    <p>With Go installed (see <code>go.mod</code> for the version), build the release configuration yourself:</p>
    <Code label="shell" code={installSource} />
    <p>
      OwnCI keeps its data in <code>~/.local/share/ownci</code>. <code>--data-dir</code>, <code>$OWNCI_DATA_DIR</code> or <code>$XDG_DATA_HOME/ownci</code> override it. On WSL, keep it on
      the Linux filesystem, not under <code>/mnt/c</code>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="docs-section" id="quick-start">
    <p class="section-kicker">Step 3</p>
    <h2>Set up and start</h2>
    <Code label="shell" code={quickStart} />
    <p>
      <code>ownci doctor</code> is read-only: it checks the platform, your user, the data directory,
      ICU, Docker and the isolation settings, and exits with code 3 if something fails.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="docs-section" id="first-workflow">
    <p class="section-kicker">Step 4</p>
    <h2>Point a workflow at your runner</h2>
    <p>In host mode (the default), <strong>both lines are required</strong>:</p>
    <Code label=".github/workflows/ci.yml" code={workflow} />
    <p>
      Open a pull request and watch the check run on your machine. For workflows without a <code>container:</code> line, see <a href={href('/docs/workflows/')}>container-runner mode</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="docs-section" id="stop">
    <p class="section-kicker">Step 5</p>
    <h2>Stop or remove it</h2>
    <Code label="shell" code={stopping} />
    <p>
      <code>ownci uninstall</code> without flags only stops the runner and explains what is left.
      See <a href={href('/docs/commands/')}>all commands</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</DocsShell>
